      "id": "IS03",
      "type": "irony",
      "got_intended": true,
      "got_literal": false,
      "model_answer": "The speaker\u2019s comment\u2014**\"Smooth move, Grace Kelly!\"**\u2014after your friend drops and breaks a plate carries several layers of meaning, blending humor, sarcasm, and social commentary. Here\u2019s a breakdown of the **literal meaning** and the **true intended communication**:\n\n### **Literal Meaning:**\n- *\"Smooth move\"* could simply mean:\n  - *\"You executed the action well\"* (e.g., \"You dropped the plate with precision\").\n  - *\"You\u2019re a natural at this\"* (implying the friend is skilled at handling objects).\n- *\"Grace Kelly\"* is a reference to the iconic actress (and former princess) known for her poise, elegance, and effortless grace. The speaker might be:\n  - Mocking the friend\u2019s clumsiness by comparing them to a \"smooth\" celebrity.\n  - Using the name as a playful insult (e.g., \"You\u2019re as graceful as Grace Kelly when you\u2019re *not*\").\n\n### **True Intended Meaning (Sarcasm & Subtext):**\nThe comment is **highly sarcastic** and likely carries these deeper implications:\n1. **Mocking the Friend\u2019s Clumsiness:**\n   - The speaker is **mocking the friend\u2019s lack of skill** in handling objects. Instead of saying *\"You dropped it!\"* or *\"You\u2019re clumsy!\"*, they use a complimentary phrase to **dodge accountability** while still criticizing.\n   - The contrast between *\"smooth move\"* (a positive phrase) and *\"Grace Kelly\"* (a reference to someone *not* known for dropping things) is deliberate.\n\n2. **Social Pressure & Blame-Shifting:**\n   - The comment avoids directly admitting fault, which could be seen as **defensive or dismissive**. It\u2019s a way to **soften the criticism** while still implying the friend is at fault.\n   - In some contexts, this could be a **subtle way to say \"You\u2019re not as careful as you think you are.\"**\n\n3. **Cultural/Historical Reference:**\n   - Grace Kelly\u2019s reputation for elegance and grace makes her an **ironic contrast** to the broken plate. The speaker might be:\n     - **Mocking the friend\u2019s inability to match her standards** (e.g., \"You\u2019re not graceful like Grace Kelly\").\n     - **Using the name as a way to make the criticism more absurd** (e.g., \"You\u2019re as graceful as a princess who *doesn\u2019t* drop things\").\n\n4. **Possible Underlying Tone:**\n   - **Playful teasing** (if the friend is close and the speaker is joking).\n   - **Subtle criticism** (if the speaker is annoyed or frustrated).\n   - **A way to avoid confrontation** (if they don\u2019t want to say *\"You broke it!\"* directly).\n\n### **Why This Works as Sarcasm:**\nSarcasm thrives on **contrasting what is said with what is meant**. Here, the speaker:\n- Uses a **positive phrase** (*\"smooth move\"*) to **mask a negative implication**.\n- References **Grace Kelly**, a figure associated with **effortless elegance**, to **highlight the opposite** (the broken plate).\n- Avoids **direct blame**, making the criticism **more subtle and potentially hurtful** if taken the wrong way.\n\n### **How to Respond (If You Were the Friend):**\nDepending on the tone, you might:\n- **Laugh it off:** *\"Oh, you\u2019re just saying that because I\u2019m not Grace Kelly!\"*\n- **Take it seriously:** *\"Wow, thanks for the compliment\u2014you\u2019re really the smoothest person I know.\"*\n- **Clarify:** *\"Wait, you\u2019re saying I\u2019m clumsy? That\u2019s not how I see it!\"*\n\n### **Final Verdict:**\nThe speaker is **sarcastically mocking the friend\u2019s clumsiness** by framing it as a compliment, using Grace Kelly as a **deliberate contrast**. The **true meaning** is likely:\n*\"You\u2019re not as graceful or careful as you think you are\u2014your \u2018smooth move\u2019 was actually a disaster.\"*\n\nWould you like help tailoring a response based on the dynamic between you and the speaker?",
      "marked_literal": false
