      "got_intended": true,
      "got_literal": false,
      "model_answer": "In this situation, **B\u2019s literal meaning** is straightforward: *\"He did break your favorite vase last time.\"* This is a factual statement about Tom\u2019s past behavior.\n\nHowever, **B\u2019s true intended meaning**\u2014what they are actually trying to communicate\u2014goes beyond the literal statement. Here\u2019s what\u2019s likely being implied:\n\n1. **Avoidance of Conflict or Guilt Tripping** \u2013 B is subtly suggesting that inviting Tom would be a bad idea because it might lead to another argument or awkwardness. They\u2019re not directly saying *\"Don\u2019t invite him because he\u2019s a jerk,\"* but they\u2019re hinting that doing so could cause trouble.\n\n2. **Conditional or Hypothetical Rejection** \u2013 The phrasing *\"Well, he did break your favorite vase last time\"* sets up a **conditional** (\"If you invite him, you might regret it because...\"). It\u2019s a way to say, *\"Given what happened before, I don\u2019t think you should invite him.\"*\n\n3. **Social Pressure or Indirect Advice** \u2013 B is essentially saying, *\"I\u2019ve seen how he acts, and I don\u2019t think you should invite him.\"* They\u2019re not being confrontational but are using past behavior as a reason to discourage the invitation.\n\n4. **Avoiding a Repeat of the Past** \u2013 The speaker might be trying to prevent a similar incident from happening again, framing it as a cautionary note rather than a direct accusation.\n\n### **Key Takeaway:**\nB is **not just stating a fact** but is **subtly discouraging the invitation** by referencing past behavior. Their tone suggests they\u2019re offering a reason why Tom shouldn\u2019t be included\u2014likely to avoid another conflict or awkward moment.\n\nWould you like to explore how this might play out in a conversation?",
