      "got_intended": false,
      "got_literal": true,
      "model_answer": "In this context, when **B** responds with *\"She has a lovely stage presence,\"* they are **not directly addressing the literal question about Sarah\u2019s singing ability** (e.g., her vocal technique, pitch, or musical skill).\n\n### **Literal Meaning:**\n- *\"Stage presence\"* refers to a performer\u2019s ability to engage an audience, command attention, and convey emotion through body language, vocal projection, and overall charisma. It is a **performance quality**, not a direct evaluation of singing ability itself.\n\n### **True Intended Meaning (Implication):**\nB is **subtly suggesting that Sarah\u2019s singing is strong enough to warrant praise for her stage presence**\u2014implying that her singing is likely **good enough to be noticed or appreciated in a performance setting**. In other words:\n- *\"She sings well enough that her stage presence is a standout quality.\"*\n- *\"Her singing is competent or impressive, which is why her presence is so engaging.\"*\n\n### **Why This Matters:**\n- The response avoids a direct answer to *\"Is Sarah a good singer?\"* but **implies that her singing is worthy of being evaluated positively** (at least in a performance context).\n- It could also be a **complimentary deflection**\u2014B might not want to overstate her singing ability but still acknowledge that her performance is engaging.\n\n### **Possible Nuances:**\n- If B is **critically assessing**, they might mean: *\"Her singing is fine, but her stage presence is the real strength.\"*\n- If B is **flattering**, they might mean: *\"Her singing is so good that her stage presence is a natural extension of it.\"*\n\nIn short, **B is not answering the question directly but is saying that Sarah\u2019s singing is good enough to make her stage presence stand out.**",
